id: design.health-checking
kind: design
title: Health checking
aliases: health check, readiness, liveness, health endpoint
tags: operations, reliability
questions:
  - what should /healthz return?
invariant: Separate liveness (should this process be restarted) from readiness (should traffic be sent here), and keep both cheap and dependency-aware in the right direction.
answer_30s: Expose two endpoints: a liveness that only checks the process itself, and a readiness that reports whether traffic can be served right now, including required dependencies.
answer_2m: |
  The split is the whole design. Liveness must not check external
  dependencies, or a database blip restarts every replica and makes the outage
  worse. Readiness should check what the request path needs -- and the honest
  version is deeper than "is the pool connected": a readiness that embeds a
  cheap dependency probe stops sending traffic to replicas that cannot serve,
  which is what makes rolling updates safe and what absorbs a partial outage. A
  third endpoint for startup (or a startup probe) covers slow boots. What not to
  do: authenticate health endpoints (so probes fail on auth changes), put slow
  work in them (probes time out under load), or expose internal details in the
  body (leak surface).
when_to_use:
  - every service behind a load balancer or orchestrator; required for zero-downtime deploys
failure_modes:
  - readiness with a hard dependency on a flaky service, flapping all replicas out of rotation
  - liveness that restarts the fleet when shared infrastructure is slow
  - health endpoints behind auth or rate limits, so probes fail during incidents
related:
  - k8s.probe.readiness
  - k8s.probe.liveness
  - k8s.probe.startup
  - design.graceful-shutdown
  - design.observability
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-liveness-readiness-startup-probes/
